{"3.1.1":"<h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Before running formal statistical tests, it helps to know <strong>where</strong> the variation is coming from. Is it inside each item, between items made one after another, or over time? A multi-vari study answers that with a simple chart, and it often points straight at the area hiding the main cause. It saves teams from testing dozens of possible causes in the wrong place.</p><h4>The big idea</h4><p>Variation can be sorted into <strong>families</strong>. The three classic ones:</p><ol><li><strong>Within-piece (positional) variation</strong>: differences <strong>inside a single unit</strong>. A trailer floor thicker at the front than the back; a pallet heavier on one side; one tire worn more than the others on the same truck.</li><li><strong>Piece-to-piece (cyclical) variation</strong>: differences <strong>between consecutive units</strong> made under the same conditions. Load 1, load 2 and load 3 on the same dock in the same hour take different times.</li><li><strong>Time-to-time (temporal) variation</strong>: differences <strong>between time periods</strong>: hour to hour, shift to shift, day to day, batch to batch. Monday morning versus Friday afternoon.</li></ol><p>A multi-vari study measures enough of each so you can see which family is <strong>largest</strong>. The largest family is where to look for the dominant cause (sometimes called the <strong>Red X</strong>).</p><h4>How to run one</h4><ol><li>Pick time periods (for example, three different hours or shifts).</li><li>In each period, take several consecutive pieces (for example, 3 to 5 in a row).</li><li>On each piece, measure at several positions if within-piece variation is possible.</li><li>Plot everything on a <strong>multi-vari chart</strong>: positions within a piece joined by a vertical line, piece averages joined within each period, and period averages joined across time.</li><li>Compare the sizes of the three kinds of spread.</li></ol><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 190\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Multi-vari chart: three hours, three pieces each, three positions per piece. Positions within a piece and pieces within an hour vary little, but the hourly averages jump a lot, so time-to-time variation dominates.\">\n<line class=\"d-axis\" x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"168.0\" x2=\"350.0\" y2=\"168.0\"/>\n<line class=\"d-axis\" x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"20.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"168.0\"/>\n<line class=\"d-thin\" x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"148.1\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"142.3\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"50.0\" cy=\"148.1\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"50.0\" cy=\"145.2\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"50.0\" cy=\"142.3\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<line class=\"d-thin\" x1=\"80.0\" y1=\"140.4\" x2=\"80.0\" y2=\"134.8\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"80.0\" cy=\"140.4\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"80.0\" cy=\"137.6\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"80.0\" cy=\"134.8\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<line class=\"d-thin\" x1=\"110.0\" y1=\"138.6\" x2=\"110.0\" y2=\"132.8\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"110.0\" cy=\"138.6\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"110.0\" cy=\"135.7\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"110.0\" cy=\"132.8\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<path class=\"d-dash\" d=\"M50.0,145.2 L80.0,137.6 L110.0,135.7\"/>\n<text class=\"d-small d-muted\" x=\"80.0\" y=\"182.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hour 1</text>\n<line class=\"d-thin\" x1=\"156.0\" y1=\"106.2\" x2=\"156.0\" y2=\"100.5\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"156.0\" cy=\"106.2\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"156.0\" cy=\"103.4\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"156.0\" cy=\"100.5\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<line class=\"d-thin\" x1=\"186.0\" y1=\"98.7\" x2=\"186.0\" y2=\"93.0\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"186.0\" cy=\"98.7\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"186.0\" cy=\"95.8\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"186.0\" cy=\"93.0\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<line class=\"d-thin\" x1=\"216.0\" y1=\"96.8\" x2=\"216.0\" y2=\"91.0\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"216.0\" cy=\"96.8\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"216.0\" cy=\"93.9\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"216.0\" cy=\"91.0\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<path class=\"d-dash\" d=\"M156.0,103.4 L186.0,95.8 L216.0,93.9\"/>\n<text class=\"d-small d-muted\" x=\"186.0\" y=\"182.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hour 2</text>\n<line class=\"d-thin\" x1=\"262.0\" y1=\"132.8\" x2=\"262.0\" y2=\"127.2\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"262.0\" cy=\"132.8\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"262.0\" cy=\"130.0\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"262.0\" cy=\"127.2\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<line class=\"d-thin\" x1=\"292.0\" y1=\"125.2\" x2=\"292.0\" y2=\"119.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"292.0\" cy=\"125.2\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"292.0\" cy=\"122.4\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"292.0\" cy=\"119.6\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<line class=\"d-thin\" x1=\"322.0\" y1=\"123.3\" x2=\"322.0\" y2=\"117.7\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"322.0\" cy=\"123.3\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"322.0\" cy=\"120.5\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<circle class=\"d-dot\" cx=\"322.0\" cy=\"117.7\" r=\"2.6\"/>\n<path class=\"d-dash\" d=\"M262.0,130.0 L292.0,122.4 L322.0,120.5\"/>\n<text class=\"d-small d-muted\" x=\"292.0\" y=\"182.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hour 3</text>\n<path class=\"d-line\" d=\"M80.0,139.5 L186.0,97.7 L292.0,124.3\"/>\n<rect class=\"d-box-strong\" x=\"76.0\" y=\"135.5\" width=\"8.0\" height=\"8.0\" rx=\"1\"/>\n<rect class=\"d-box-strong\" x=\"182.0\" y=\"93.7\" width=\"8.0\" height=\"8.0\" rx=\"1\"/>\n<rect class=\"d-box-strong\" x=\"288.0\" y=\"120.3\" width=\"8.0\" height=\"8.0\" rx=\"1\"/>\n<text class=\"d-small d-muted\" x=\"34.0\" y=\"16.0\">Vertical line = one piece (3 positions)</text>\n<text class=\"d-small d-data-text\" x=\"34.0\" y=\"30.0\">Squares joined = hour-to-hour averages</text>\n</svg></figure><p><em>The short vertical lines (within piece) and the small wiggles between pieces are tiny compared with the jumps between hours.</em></p><h4>Reading the chart</h4><ul><li><strong>Long vertical lines</strong> (big spread inside each piece) → <strong>within-piece</strong> variation dominates. Look at things that differ across one unit: fixture alignment, uneven heating, loading pattern.</li><li><strong>Big differences between consecutive pieces</strong> in the same period → <strong>piece-to-piece</strong> variation dominates. Look at things that change unit to unit: material lots, operator technique per unit, cycle-to-cycle machine behavior.</li><li><strong>Big jumps between period averages</strong> → <strong>time-to-time</strong> variation dominates. Look at things that change over time: shift change, temperature through the day, tool wear, supplier deliveries, staffing levels.</li></ul><p>In the example chart, the hour-to-hour averages jump by 20 units while pieces and positions vary by only 2 to 5. The team should investigate what changes between hours.</p><h4>Beyond the chart</h4><p>The same idea can be quantified with <strong>nested ANOVA</strong> or <strong>variance components</strong> analysis, which estimate how much of the total variance each family contributes (for example, 70% time-to-time, 20% piece-to-piece, 10% within-piece). That is more precise but the chart alone usually makes the answer obvious.</p><h4>Common mistakes and exam traps</h4><ul><li><strong>Multi-vari is for finding the family of variation, before formal tests.</strong> It does not prove a specific cause.</li><li><strong>Sample across enough time</strong> to capture the time-to-time family; a study done in one hour cannot see it.</li><li><strong>Identify the dominant family</strong> from the chart by comparing the size of each kind of spread.</li></ul><h4>Check yourself</h4><ol><li>Name the three families of variation in a multi-vari study.</li><li>A chart shows long vertical lines for each piece, but piece averages and hourly averages barely change. Which family dominates?</li><li>Why should a multi-vari study span several time periods?</li><li>Which statistical technique quantifies the same breakdown?</li><li>Does a multi-vari chart identify the root cause?</li></ol><details class=\"answers\"><summary>Show answers</summary><ol><li>Within-piece (positional), piece-to-piece (cyclical), time-to-time (temporal).</li><li>Within-piece.</li><li>Otherwise time-to-time variation cannot appear in the data.</li><li>Nested ANOVA (variance components).</li><li>No. It narrows down where the cause lives; further investigation and tests identify it.</li></ol></details><h4>Key takeaways</h4><ul><li>Families: within-piece, piece-to-piece, time-to-time.</li><li>Sample several pieces per period, several periods, several positions per piece.</li><li>The largest family on the chart shows where to look for the main cause.</li><li>Variance components (nested ANOVA) quantify the same thing.</li></ul>","3.1.2":"<h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A <strong>distribution</strong> describes how values of a variable are spread out: which values are common and which are rare. Different kinds of data follow different distributions, and the right distribution determines the right calculations, charts and tests. Exam questions often describe a situation and ask which distribution fits.</p><h4>The big idea</h4><p>Distributions come in two families, matching the two data types from 2.2.1:</p><ul><li><strong>Continuous distributions</strong> for measured data: normal, lognormal, Weibull, exponential, uniform.</li><li><strong>Discrete distributions</strong> for counts: binomial, Poisson, hypergeometric.</li></ul><p>A third group, <strong>sampling distributions</strong> (t, chi-square, F), describe statistics calculated from samples. They are the engines behind hypothesis tests.</p><h4>Continuous distributions</h4><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 200\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Four continuous distribution shapes: symmetric normal, exponential falling from a peak at zero, right-skewed lognormal, and flat uniform.\">\n<line class=\"d-axis\" x1=\"12.0\" y1=\"88.0\" x2=\"170.0\" y2=\"88.0\"/>\n<path class=\"d-line\" d=\"M15.9,87.8 L19.9,87.7 L23.9,87.4 L27.8,87.1 L31.8,86.5 L35.7,85.5 L39.6,84.2 L43.6,82.4 L47.6,79.9 L51.5,76.6 L55.5,72.6 L59.4,67.8 L63.4,62.4 L67.3,56.5 L71.2,50.4 L75.2,44.6 L79.1,39.5 L83.1,35.5 L87.0,32.9 L91.0,32.0 L95.0,32.9 L98.9,35.5 L102.8,39.5 L106.8,44.6 L110.8,50.4 L114.7,56.5 L118.7,62.4 L122.6,67.8 L126.5,72.6 L130.5,76.6 L134.4,79.9 L138.4,82.4 L142.3,84.2 L146.3,85.5 L150.2,86.5 L154.2,87.1 L158.2,87.4 L162.1,87.7 L166.0,87.8 L170.0,87.9 L173.9,88.0 L177.9,88.0 L181.8,88.0 L185.8,88.0 L189.8,88.0 L193.7,88.0 L197.7,88.0 L201.6,88.0 L205.6,88.0 L209.5,88.0 L213.4,88.0 L217.4,88.0 L221.3,88.0 L225.3,88.0 L229.2,88.0 L233.2,88.0 L237.2,88.0 L241.1,88.0 L245.1,88.0 L249.0,88.0 L252.9,88.0 L256.9,88.0 L260.9,88.0 L264.8,88.0 L268.8,88.0 L272.7,88.0 L276.7,88.0 L280.6,88.0 L284.6,88.0 L288.5,88.0 L292.4,88.0 L296.4,88.0 L300.3,88.0 L304.3,88.0 L308.2,88.0 L312.2,88.0 L316.2,88.0 L320.1,88.0 L324.1,88.0 L328.0,88.0 L331.9,88.0 L335.9,88.0 L339.9,88.0 L343.8,88.0 L347.8,88.0 L351.7,88.0 L355.6,88.0 L359.6,88.0 L363.6,88.0 L367.5,88.0 L371.4,88.0 L375.4,88.0 L379.4,88.0 L383.3,88.0 L387.2,88.0 L391.2,88.0 L395.1,88.0 L399.1,88.0 L403.1,88.0 L407.0,88.0 L410.9,88.0 L414.9,88.0 L418.9,88.0 L422.8,88.0 L426.8,88.0 L430.7,88.0 L434.6,88.0 L438.6,88.0 L442.6,88.0 L446.5,88.0 L450.4,88.0 L454.4,88.0 L458.4,88.0 L462.3,88.0 L466.2,88.0 L470.2,88.0 L474.1,88.0 L478.1,88.0 L482.1,88.0 L486.0,88.0 L489.9,88.0 L493.9,88.0 L497.9,88.0 L501.8,88.0 L505.8,88.0 L509.7,88.0 L513.6,88.0 L517.6,88.0 L521.5,88.0 L525.5,88.0 L529.4,88.0 L533.4,88.0 L537.4,88.0 L541.3,88.0 L545.2,88.0 L549.2,88.0 L553.1,88.0 L557.1,88.0 L561.1,88.0 L565.0,88.0 L568.9,88.0 L572.9,88.0 L576.9,88.0 L580.8,88.0 L584.8,88.0 L588.7,88.0 L592.6,88.0 L596.6,88.0 L600.6,88.0 L604.5,88.0 L608.4,88.0 L612.4,88.0 L616.4,88.0 L620.3,88.0 L624.2,88.0 L628.2,88.0 L632.1,88.0 L636.1,88.0 L640.1,8