8.4,180.0 L792.4,180.0 L796.3,180.0 L800.2,180.0 L804.2,180.0 L808.1,180.0 L812.1,180.0 L816.1,180.0 L820.0,180.0 L824.0,180.0 L827.9,180.0 L831.9,180.0 L835.8,180.0 L839.8,180.0 L843.7,180.0 L847.6,180.0 L851.6,180.0 L855.5,180.0 L859.5,180.0 L863.5,180.0 L867.4,180.0 L871.4,180.0 L875.3,180.0 L879.2,180.0 L883.2,180.0 L887.1,180.0 L891.1,180.0 L895.0,180.0 L899.0,180.0 L903.0,180.0 L906.9,180.0 L910.9,180.0 L914.8,180.0 L918.8,180.0 L922.7,180.0 L926.6,180.0 L930.6,180.0 L934.5,180.0 L938.5,180.0 L942.5,180.0 L946.4,180.0 L950.4,180.0 L954.3,180.0 L958.2,180.0 L962.2,180.0 L966.1,180.0 L970.1,180.0 L974.0,180.0 L978.0,180.0\"/>\n<text class=\"d-small d-strong\" x=\"188.0\" y=\"116.0\">Uniform</text>\n</svg></figure><p><em>Shape is the quickest clue to which distribution fits.</em></p><ul><li><strong>Normal</strong>: symmetric bell. Many measurements that result from lots of small independent influences (fill weights, dimensions).</li><li><strong>Lognormal</strong>: right-skewed; the logarithm of the data is normal. Positive values with a long right tail: repair times, delivery delays, income.</li><li><strong>Weibull</strong>: very flexible; used for <strong>time to failure</strong> (life data). Its <strong>shape parameter β</strong> tells the failure pattern: β below 1 = early-life failures (\"infant mortality\", failure rate falling); β = 1 = random failures (constant rate, same as exponential); β above 1 = wear-out (failure rate rising).</li><li><strong>Exponential</strong>: <strong>time between random events</strong> that happen at a constant rate: time between breakdowns, between customer arrivals. It is <strong>memoryless</strong>: the chance of failing in the next hour does not depend on how long the item has already lasted.</li><li><strong>Uniform</strong>: every value in a range equally likely (a random number from 0 to 1).</li></ul><p>Example (exponential): a reefer unit fails on average once every 200 operating hours, at random. The chance it fails within the next 100 hours = 1 − e^(−100/200) = 1 − e^(−0.5) ≈ <strong>0.39</strong>.</p><h4>Discrete distributions</h4><p><strong>Binomial</strong>: the number of \"successes\" (often defectives) in a <strong>fixed number n</strong> of <strong>independent</strong> trials, each with the <strong>same probability p</strong>.</p><div class=\"formula\">P(x) = nCx × pˣ × (1 − p)ⁿ⁻ˣ        mean = np</div><p>where nCx = n! ÷ [x!(n − x)!] is the number of ways to choose x items from n.</p><p>Example: 5% of invoices have errors. In a sample of 20: P(0 errors) = 0.95²⁰ ≈ <strong>0.358</strong>; P(exactly 1) = 20 × 0.05 × 0.95¹⁹ ≈ <strong>0.377</strong>; P(2 or more) = 1 − 0.358 − 0.377 ≈ <strong>0.264</strong>. The average number expected is np = 20 × 0.05 = <strong>1</strong>.</p><p><strong>Poisson</strong>: the number of events (often defects) in a fixed amount of time, space or units, when events occur independently at a constant average rate <strong>λ</strong>.</p><div class=\"formula\">P(x) = e^(−λ) × λˣ ÷ x!        mean = variance = λ</div><p>Example: a dock averages λ = 3 forklift incidents a month. P(0 in a month) = e^(−3) ≈ <strong>0.050</strong>; P(exactly 2) = e^(−3) × 3² ÷ 2 ≈ <strong>0.224</strong>.</p><p>A Poisson distribution's mean and variance are equal, which is a handy check: if counts vary much more than their average, the data are \"over-dispersed\" and something else is going on.</p><p><strong>Hypergeometric</strong>: like the binomial, but <strong>sampling without replacement</strong> from a <strong>small</strong> population, so each draw changes the odds for the next. Example: picking 5 cartons from a lot of 40 that contains 4 damaged ones. When the sample is small compared with the population (less than about 5%), the binomial is a good approximation.</p><h4>Matching data to distributions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Distribution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Number of defective loads in a sample of 50</td><td>Binomial</td></tr><tr><td>Number of defects on one invoice, or per 100 miles</td><td>Poisson</td></tr><tr><td>Drawing samples from a small lot without putting them back</td><td>Hypergeometric</td></tr><tr><td>Time between equipment failures (random)</td><td>Exponential</td></tr><tr><td>Time to failure with infant mortality or wear-out</td><td>Weibull</td></tr><tr><td>Repair time with a long right tail</td><td>Lognormal (often)</td></tr><tr><td>Fill weight, part dimension</td><td>Normal (often)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Approximations</h4><ul><li><strong>Poisson approximates binomial</strong> when n is large and p is small (rare events).</li><li><strong>Normal approximates binomial</strong> when both np and n(1 − p) are at least 5 (some texts use 10). With n = 200 and p = 0.12, np = 24 and n(1 − p) = 176, so the normal approximation is fine.</li></ul><h4>Sampling distributions</h4><p>These are not used to model raw data, but to describe statistics computed from samples:</p><ul><li><strong>t distribution</strong>: like the normal but with <strong>heavier tails</strong>, used when σ is estimated from the sample (s). Its shape depends on <strong>degrees of freedom</strong> (df, usually n − 1); with large df it becomes the normal. Used for t-tests and confidence intervals for means.</li><li><strong>Chi-square (χ²) distribution</strong>: right-skewed, used for tests about a <strong>variance</strong> and for contingency tables.</li><li><strong>F distribution</strong>: the ratio of two variances, used to compare variances and in ANOVA.</li></ul><h4>Common mistakes and exam traps</h4><ul><li><strong>Defects on a unit → Poisson; defectives in a sample → binomial.</strong></li><li><strong>Time between events → exponential; time to failure with changing rate → Weibull.</strong></li><li><strong>Poisson mean = variance = λ.</strong></li><li><strong>Binomial needs fixed n, independent trials, constant p.</strong></li><li><strong>Without replacement from a small population → hypergeometric.</strong></li></ul><h4>Check yourself</h4><ol><li>Which distribution models the number of scratches on a painted trailer panel?</li><li>What does a Weibull shape parameter β above 1 indicate?</li><li>10% of pallets are mis-labeled. In 10 pallets, what is the probability none are mis-labeled?</li><li>A Poisson process averages 2 events per hour. What is the variance of the hourly count?</li><li>Which sampling distribution is used to compare two variances?</li></ol><details class=\"answers\"><summary>Show answers</summary><ol><li>Poisson (defect counts on a unit).</li><li>Wear-out: the failure rate increases with age.</li><li>0.9¹⁰ ≈ 0.349.</li><li>2 (variance equals the mean).</li><li>The F distribution.</li></ol></details><h4>Key takeaways</h4><ul><li>Continuous: normal (symmetric), lognormal (right skew), Weibull (life data, β sets the pattern), exponential (time between random events, memoryless), uniform.</li><li>Discrete: binomial (defectives, fixed n, constant p), Poisson (defects, mean = variance = λ), hypergeometric (without replacement, small population).</li><li>Sampling distributions: t (means, σ unknown), χ² (variances, contingency tables), F (variance ratios, ANOVA).</li></ul>"}